    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d239">

                <pb facs="0254" n="246" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg246"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9156: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-04T12:00:00+08:00">August 4,
                        1937—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received August 4—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>373. Embassy here has repeated to Hankow by radio dated August 4, 11 a.m., the
                    first sentence of Tientsin’s 11, August 3, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="36"
                        xml:id="d239-f1"/> and all of Tientsin’s 12, August 3, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d239-f2"/> with the following statement.</p>
                <pb facs="0255" n="247" xml:id="pg247"/>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“All places named above are in Hopei Province. Telegraph communication with
                        them from Peiping and Tientsin is broken but it is believed that telegraph
                        is open from Hankow. Unless you have received other instructions from
                        Nanking please telegraph immediately to Americans concerned, warning them of
                        danger from Peiping military activities and advising their withdrawal to
                        places of safety.”<anchor n="38" xml:id="d239-f3"/></p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d239-f1" n="36" xml:id="d239-f1-content">
                <p><ref target="frus1937v03#pg_316"><hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.</hi>, p.
                    316</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d239-f2" n="37" xml:id="d239-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d239-f3" n="38" xml:id="d239-f3-content">
                <p>As the conflict spread, similar warnings to withdraw to places of safety, and
                    later to leave China, were sent to Americans in places considered dangerous. In
                    many cases the Americans concerned chose to remain. Numerous telegrams reporting
                    on the carrying out of the policy of evacuation are not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
